# aifirstspec/ — Package Memory Convention

`aifirstspec/` is the committed, agent-readable **package-memory store** for NSO
service packages, maintained by the **aifirst CLI** (`aifirst spec …`). It is the
durable, cross-session source of truth for **what packages exist, from which spec
they were built, and what changed since** — so a fresh session can tell a **new**
package apart from a **modification** of an existing one.

> **Minimum CLI version:** `aifirst spec` ships in the first aifirst release
> **after `v1.0.3-alpha`**. Detect support by probing for the subcommand (e.g.
> `aifirst spec` prints its usage) rather than parsing a version string. If the
> installed CLI lacks the `spec` subcommand, degrade gracefully (read
> `aifirstspec/packages-registry.md` directly) and warn that memory features are
> limited — never block a build on package memory.

> **Authoritative command surface:** the CLI evolves, so the **live usage output
> of `aifirst spec` (and `aifirst spec change`) is the source of truth** for
> flags and verbs. The command tables below are a convenience snapshot — if a
> command errors on usage (exit `2`), re-check the live usage rather than trusting
> this file. Do **not** copy the CLI's own reference (`docs/spec-commands.md` in
> the aifirst_cli repo) into any skill; a second copy only drifts.

## Store layout

```
aifirstspec/
  specs/<package-id>/spec.md     # living as-built truth for one package
  changes/<date>-<slug>/         # one build/modify run
    proposal.md  tasks.md  spec-snapshot.yaml   # immutable snapshot as built
  changes/archive/               # completed, dated, PR-linked runs
  packages-registry.md           # index: id → service/variant → status → change → PR → provenance → confidence
  config.yaml                    # detected packages_root, etc.
```

- **Owned by aifirst, not openspec.** Do not run the `openspec` binary against
  `aifirstspec/`; only `aifirst spec …` reads and writes it.
- **Package identity** is deterministic: `kebab(service_name)-kebab(subtype)`
  (e.g. `l3vpn-full-mesh`). It is the join key across the registry, planner, and
  backfill.
- **Snapshots are immutable** and record instance-variable **keys only** — never
  secret/credential values.

## The CLI ↔ skill contract

All deterministic work is the CLI's; the skill only branches on results. Commands
share an exit-code contract: `0` ok · `1` error · `2` usage · `3` cancelled ·
`5` identity collision.

| Command | Use |
|---|---|
| `aifirst spec init` | scaffold the store (idempotent; also run on `aifirst install`) |
| `aifirst spec discover` | brownfield: find packages by `package-meta-data.xml`, detect packages-root |
| `aifirst spec backfill --shallow` | seed stub as-built specs for discovered packages (`confidence: low`) |
| `aifirst spec plan --spec <yaml>` | per-package **NEW / MODIFY / NOOP** as JSON |
| `aifirst spec view` / `show <id>` | orient on project state (dashboard / one package) |
| `aifirst spec change new --spec <yaml> [--name <slug>]` | open **one** change covering every variant in the spec + freeze snapshot |
| `aifirst spec change archive <name> --pr <url> --status <s>` | close that change, update living specs + registry |

## How to use it in a build

1. `aifirst spec init` (idempotent).
2. First run on an existing repo → `aifirst spec discover` + `aifirst spec
   backfill --shallow` to record current state.
3. `aifirst spec plan --spec <spec>` → for each package: **NEW** = scaffold fresh;
   **MODIFY** = edit the existing package in place (deep-backfill its stored spec
   from YANG/templates first if it is a shallow `confidence: low` stub); **NOOP**
   = skip.
   - **Reconcile `NEW` against in-flight work.** `plan` diffs against the durable
     registry, which is only written at **archive** — so a package whose build is
     still open reads as `NEW` even though it exists on disk under an active
     change. Before scaffolding a `NEW`, run `aifirst spec view` and check
     **Active changes**; if one already covers this spec's service, **resume it**
     (`aifirst spec change new --spec <spec> --force`) and edit in place instead of
     re-scaffolding. (Branch on `plan`'s `active_change` field directly once the
     CLI provides it.)
4. `aifirst spec change new --spec <spec>` **once** before building — it covers
   every variant in the spec and is keyed by a change `<name>` (defaults to the
   kebab-cased service name(s); pass `--name <slug>` to override). A change name
   allows only one active instance — re-raise with `--force` if the spec changed.
   After a green, published build, `aifirst spec change archive <name> --pr <url>
   --status deployed` **once** to close it.

